gaaL(){return"Fill white cells with 1-9: each run must add up to its clue, with no repeated digit."},
gKW(){return"Shikaku"},
gWn(){return"Divide the grid into rectangles."},
gWo(){return"Each number is the area of the rectangle containing it. Split the whole grid into rectangles, one number per rectangle."},
gSU(){return"Hitori"},
ga9K(){return"Shade duplicates without isolating cells."},
ga9L(){return"Shade cells so no visible number repeats in a row or column. Two shaded cells never touch orthogonally, and unshaded cells stay connected."},
gSQ(){return"Hashi"},
ga9C(){return"Connect islands with bridges."},
ga9D(){return"Connect islands with horizontal or vertical bridges (at most 2 between two islands, no crossing). An island number = the bridges reaching it. All islands must be connected."},
gL3(){return"Slitherlink"},
gWB(){return"Draw a single loop matching the numbers."},
gWC(){return"Draw a single closed loop on the segments. Each number tells how many of its 4 sides are part of the loop."},
gTH(){return"Nurikabe"},
gabo(){return"Form islands and one connected sea."},
gabp(){return"Each number is a white island of that size. Islands never touch. Black cells form one connected sea with no 2x2 block."},
gKF(){return"Samurai"},
gVP(){return"Five overlapping 9x9 grids."},
gVQ(){return"Five 9x9 Sudokus overlap in a cross. Each grid follows the classic rules; shared areas must satisfy both grids."},
a9M(a){switch(a){case"sudoku":return"Find a row, column or 3x3 box that is nearly full: the missing digit drops in. Use notes to track a cell's candidates. Everything is deducible, no guessing needed."
case"tectonic":return"Each region holds 1 to its size, once each. Equal digits never touch, even diagonally: a placed digit blocks the 8 cells around it. Start with the small regions."
case"futoshiki":return"Each digit appears once per row and column. On a sign, the bigger number is on the open side, the smaller on the point. Chain the signs (a < b < c) to force values."
case"nonogram":return"Solve each row and column on its own. A long enough block always has a forced centre wherever it sits. Cross out cells that are surely empty: as useful as filling."
case"kakuro":return"Each run is a sum with no repeats. Some sums have a single combination (4 in 2 cells = 1+3): start there. Crossing a horizontal and a vertical run narrows choices fast."
case"shikaku":return"For each number, look for rectangles of that area containing it without covering another number. When a cell has only one possible rectangle, draw it. Every cell belongs to exactly one rectangle."
case"hitori":return"A duplicate in a row or column must be shaded, but never both. If shading would isolate whites or make two blacks touch, shade the other one. Only duplicates get shaded."
case"hashi":return"An island whose number equals its maximum possible bridges fills in automatically. Keep the whole network connected: a bridge that isolates a group is forbidden. At most two bridges between two islands."
case"slitherlink":return"A 0 forbids its 4 sides. A 3 in a corner, or two adjacent 3s, force segments. Follow the loop: each dot touches 0 or 2 segments, never 1 or 3."
case"nurikabe":return"Each number is an island of exactly that size; wall it off once complete. Two numbers can't share an island. The sea never forms a full 2x2 square: that often bans a cell."
case"samurai":return"Treat the five grids as normal Sudokus. The four corners each share a 3x3 box with the centre grid: a digit there serves both. Alternate: unlocking the centre helps the corners and vice versa."
default:return""}},
